        <!-- SECTION 2: Types of Polymeric Sand -->
        <div id="types-of-sand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Types of Polymeric Sand: Which One Is Right for Your Project?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not all polymeric sand is interchangeable, and buying based on price alone is the single most common mistake we see. The right choice depends on your joint width, your paver's surface texture, and your local climate. Here's how the category breaks down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fine vs. Coarse Grain</h3>
          <p>
            Grain size determines how narrow a joint a polymeric sand can actually fill. <strong>Fine-grain formulas</strong>, like Fortress Polymeric Sand, are milled small enough to settle into hairline joints down to 1/16 inch — increasingly common on modern porcelain and thin-joint paver installations — without bridging across the gap near the surface. <strong>Coarse-grain formulas</strong>, like DOMINATOR #1, carry a higher polymer-to-sand ratio and a bulkier aggregate designed to provide real structural stability in wide flagstone or cobblestone joints up to 2-3 inches, where a fine-grain sand would simply lack the volume and strength to hold its shape as it cures. Using the wrong grain for your joint width is the most common installation failure we encountered: fine sand in a wide joint shrinks and cracks, while coarse sand bridges over a narrow joint and never fully settles to the base.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Haze-Resistant ("No-Haze") vs. Standard Formulas</h3>
          <p>
            Haze — the chalky white film that can appear on the paver surface after installation — is caused by binder residue that migrates out of the joint during misting and dries on the surrounding surface instead of staying locked in the sand. Standard-formula sands are more prone to this, especially on smooth, honed, or dark-colored pavers where any residue is highly visible. <strong>No-haze and haze-resistant formulas</strong>, like Techniseal HP NextGel and Alliance Gator Maxx G2, use binder chemistry engineered to gel and bond to the sand grains almost immediately on contact with water, dramatically reducing how much binder is available to migrate onto the paver face. If you're installing on smooth or dark pavers, or if you've been burned by haze on a previous project, paying the premium for a haze-resistant formula is worth it — haze removal after the fact often requires a specialized acid-based cleaner that can etch certain paver finishes if used incorrectly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Color Options</h3>
          <p>
            Polymeric sand is available in shades from natural sand tan through gray, charcoal, and beige-brown blends designed to disappear against specific paver colors rather than stand out as a visible grout line. Premium brands like Alliance and Unilock offer a dozen or more color-matched blends calibrated to popular paver product lines, while budget sands typically offer three to five standard tones. Consistency matters as much as selection — buy enough bags from the same production lot for your whole project, since color can shift subtly between manufacturing batches, and a visible seam between two slightly different tans is one of the most common cosmetic complaints homeowners have after a DIY installation.
          </p>

