        <!-- SECTION 5: How to Apply -->
        <div id="how-to-apply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Apply Polymeric Sand: The Complete DIY Guide
          </h2>

          <p>
            Applying polymeric sand correctly is a realistic weekend DIY project, but the process is unforgiving of shortcuts — more polymeric sand failures come from rushed application than from a bad product. Here's the process we followed for every sand in this test.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Measure Your Joint Width and Prep the Surface</h3>
          <p>
            Measure your actual joint widths before buying — this determines whether you need a fine-grain product like Fortress, a standard formula like HP NextGel, or a heavy-duty wide-joint sand like DOMINATOR #1. Make sure pavers are completely dry and free of dirt, old jointing sand, efflorescence, and organic debris before you start; polymeric sand will not bond properly to damp or dirty surfaces, and applying to a wet paver face is one of the most common causes of premature haze.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Sweep the Sand Into the Joints</h3>
          <p>
            Pour the dry sand over the paver surface and use a stiff push broom to work it into every joint, sweeping in multiple directions to fill from the base up without leaving voids. Fill joints slightly above the paver surface to account for settling during compaction, then use a leaf blower or soft broom to remove every trace of excess sand from the paver face — any sand left on the surface will contribute directly to haze during misting.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Compact the Joints</h3>
          <p>
            Run a plate compactor fitted with a protective pad over the entire surface, or hand-tamp smaller areas, to settle the sand fully to the base of each joint and eliminate air pockets. Sweep in a second, lighter application afterward to top off any joints that settled below the paver surface during compaction, then blow off the surface again before misting.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Mist to Activate</h3>
          <p>
            Set your hose nozzle to a fine mist or shower setting — never a jet stream, which will wash sand back out of the joints before it can cure. Apply water in two to three light passes across small sections at a time, allowing each pass to soak in before the next, until the joint is saturated but no water is pooling on the surface. Overwatering is the single biggest cause of haze, since excess water carries binder up out of the joint and onto the paver face as it dries.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Cure and Protect</h3>
          <p>
            Keep the area free of foot traffic for at least 24 hours and protect it from rain during that window — check your specific product's forecast requirement, since some fast-gelling formulas like HP NextGel set faster than others. Avoid pressure washing or heavy watering for at least a week to allow the polymer to reach full strength. Done correctly, a properly cured polymeric sand joint should feel firm and immovable underfoot within a day or two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Choosing by Joint Width and Climate -->
        <div id="choosing-by-climate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Choosing Polymeric Sand by Joint Width and Climate
          </h2>

          <p>
            Beyond brand and price, two factors matter more than anything else when picking a polymeric sand: how wide your joints actually are, and how harsh your local freeze-thaw cycle is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Matching Sand to Joint Width</h3>
          <p>
            As a rule of thumb, joints under 1/8 inch call for a fine-grain formula like Fortress Polymeric Sand, joints between 1/8 and 1.5 inches (the vast majority of standard concrete paver installations) are well served by an all-around formula like Gator Maxx G2 or a haze-focused one like HP NextGel, and joints wider than 1.5 inches — typical of flagstone, cobblestone, and irregular natural stone — need a heavy-duty, high-polymer formula like DOMINATOR #1 that's specifically rated for the extra volume and structural demands of a wide gap.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Freeze-Thaw Considerations</h3>
          <p>
            In climates that see repeated hard freezes and thaws each winter, joint durability depends as much on the polymer's flexibility as its raw strength. Water that seeps into a cured joint expands roughly 9% as it freezes; over dozens of cycles each winter, a rigid, brittle polymer bond can develop hairline fractures and eventually pop sand loose. Formulas engineered for flexibility, like <strong>EnviroSAND Polymeric Sand</strong>, are built to compress and expand slightly with that ice movement rather than cracking under it. If you're in USDA zones 5 and colder — the northern half of the country and most of Canada — prioritize a freeze-thaw-rated formula and pay attention to the manufacturer's minimum installation temperature, since curing in marginal cold can weaken the final bond before winter even arrives.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Drainage and Base Preparation Matter Too</h3>
          <p>
            No polymeric sand, however good, can compensate for a poorly built paver base. If water pools under or within your paver installation because of inadequate slope or a compacted base that doesn't drain, that standing water will work against even the best freeze-thaw-rated sand over time. Make sure your paver base is properly compacted and graded to shed water away from the installation before you invest in premium jointing sand — the base is doing at least as much long-term work as the sand sitting on top of it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best polymeric sand for paver joints?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Alliance Gator Maxx G2 is the best overall polymeric sand for paver joints. Its cross-linked polymer binder resists cracking and washout, its low-haze activation process leaves minimal residue, and it's rated for an unusually wide joint range of 1/16 inch to 2 inches. For dedicated haze elimination on smooth or dark pavers, Techniseal HP NextGel is the better specialist choice. For wide flagstone joints over an inch, DOMINATOR #1 Polymeric Jointing Sand provides the structural stability finer sands lack.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between fine and coarse polymeric sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fine-grain polymeric sand, like Fortress Polymeric Sand, is milled small enough to settle into narrow joints down to 1/16 inch without bridging near the surface. Coarse-grain, heavy-duty formulas, like DOMINATOR #1, carry more polymer binder and a bulkier aggregate designed to provide structural stability in wide joints up to 2-3 inches. Using fine sand in a wide joint often leads to shrinkage and cracking, while coarse sand tends to bridge over narrow joints instead of settling to the base.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop polymeric sand from hazing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Haze is caused by polymer binder migrating out of the joint and drying on the paver surface during misting. To prevent it, sweep all excess sand off the paver face before misting, mist in light, controlled passes rather than a heavy soak, and never let water pool on the surface. Choosing a haze-resistant formula such as Techniseal HP NextGel or Alliance Gator Maxx G2 also substantially reduces the risk, since their binders are engineered to lock into the sand rather than travel with the water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can polymeric sand be used on wide joints like flagstone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but not every polymeric sand is rated for it. Standard formulas are designed for joints up to about 1.5 inches and can shrink or crack in wider gaps. For flagstone, cobblestone, or other natural stone with joints from 1 to 3 inches wide, use a heavy-duty, high-polymer formula specifically rated for wide joints, such as DOMINATOR #1 Polymeric Jointing Sand, which is formulated to build strength through the full depth of an oversized joint without shrinking as it cures.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does polymeric sand last before it needs to be replaced?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A properly installed premium polymeric sand typically lasts 5-10 years before it needs to be topped off or replaced, depending on climate, traffic, and drainage. Budget formulas with lower polymer content, such as SEK Surebond SB, more realistically last 3-4 years before joints begin showing minor erosion. Freeze-thaw cycling, poor drainage, and skipping proper compaction during installation are the most common reasons polymeric sand fails earlier than expected.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What's the best temperature to install polymeric sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most polymeric sands should be installed when the surface and ambient temperature are above 50°F and there's no rain forecast for at least 24-48 hours. EnviroSAND Polymeric Sand, in particular, specifies a 50°F minimum for proper curing. Installing in temperatures that are too cold slows or prevents the polymer from activating correctly, while installing right before rain can wash out the sand before it has time to cure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to compact polymeric sand before misting it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. After sweeping polymeric sand into the joints, use a plate compactor with a protective pad, or hand-tamp for small areas, to settle the sand fully to the base of the joint and eliminate air pockets. Skipping compaction is one of the most common installation mistakes — it leaves joints under-filled, which shows up later as visible settling, cracking, or a soft, spongy feel underfoot even though the sand has technically cured.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is polymeric sand better than regular sand for paver joints?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For nearly every paver or flagstone installation, yes. Regular jointing sand has no binder, so it washes out under rain and foot traffic within a season or two, leaving gaps for weeds and ants. Polymeric sand's polymer binder cures into a semi-rigid matrix that locks the joint together, resists washout, and blocks weed germination and ant tunneling for years. The only common exception is loose-laid natural stone that needs to remain fully permeable for drainage, where regular sand or a specialized permeable jointing product may be preferred.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

